    <div class="section-title">TITLE 24—HOUSING AND URBAN DEVELOPMENT</div>
    <p>CHAPTER I—OFFICE OF ASSISTANT SECRETARY FOR EQUAL OPPORTUNITY, DEPARTMENT OF HOUSING AND URBAN DEVELOPMENT</p>
    <p>PART 135—ECONOMIC OPPORTUNITIES FOR LOW- AND VERY LOW-INCOME PERSONS</p>

    <div class="section-title">Appendix to Part 135</div>

    <div class="section-title">I. EXAMPLES OF EFFORTS TO OFFER TRAINING AND EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITIES TO SECTION 3 RESIDENTS</div>

    <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <p>(1) Entering into a "first source" hiring agreements with organizations representing Section 3 residents.</p>
        <p>(2) Sponsoring a HUD-certified "Step-Up" employment and training program for Section 3 residents.</p>
        <p>(3) Providing training programs, which are consistent with the requirements of the Department of Labor, for public and Indian housing residents and other section 3 residents.</p>
        <p>(4) Advertising the training and employment positions by distributing flyers (which include the positions to be filled, the qualifications required, and where to obtain additional information about the application process) to every occupied dwelling unit in the housing development or developments where category 1 or category 2 persons reside.</p>
        <p>(5) Posting job vacancies in Work-in-Texas or with my local Workforce Solutions Center.</p>
        <p>(6) Contacting resident councils and other resident organizations in the affected housing development to request assistance in notifying residents of the training and employment positions to be filled.</p>
        <p>(7) Sponsoring (scheduling, advertising, financing or providing in-kind services) a job informational meeting to be conducted by an HA or contractor representative or representatives at a location in the housing development or developments where category 1 or category 2 persons reside or in the neighborhood or service area of such development.</p>
        <p>(8) Arranging assistance in conducting job interviews and completing job applications for Section 3 residents.</p>
        <p>(9) Arranging for a location in the housing development or developments where category 1 or category 2 persons reside, or the neighborhood or service area of the project, to conduct job interviews and job applications.</p>
        <p>(10) Contacting agencies administering HUD YouthBuild programs, and requesting their assistance in recruiting Section 3 residents for employment opportunities.</p>
        <p>(11) Contacting agencies administering HUD programs, and requesting their assistance in recruiting Section 3 residents for employment opportunities.</p>
        <p>(12) Advertising the jobs to be filled through the local media, such as community television news, newspapers, general circulation, and radio advertising.</p>
        <p>(13) Employing a job coordinator, or contracting with a business concern that is licensed and certified to provide job coordination services, to assist in the recruitment of Section 3 residents.</p>
        <p>(14) For an HA, employing Section 3 residents directly on either a permanent or a temporary basis to perform work generated by section 3 assistance. (Type of employment is referred to as "force account labor" in HUD's Indian housing programs).</p>
        <p>(15) For an HA, contracting with a business concern to provide services to Section 3 residents.</p>
        <p>(16) Where there are more qualified Section 3 residents than there are positions to be filled, maintaining a file of eligible qualified Section 3 residents for future employment opportunities.</p>
        <p>(17) Undertaking job counseling, education and related programs in association with local educational institutions.</p>
        <p>(18) Undertaking such continued job training efforts as may be necessary to ensure the continued employment of Section 3 residents previously hired for employment opportunities.</p>
        <p>(19) After selection of bidders but prior to execution of contracts, incorporating into the contract a negotiated provision for a specific number of public housing or other Section 3 residents to be trained or employed on the section 3 covered assistance.</p>
        <p>(20) Developing plans and implementing a plan (e.g., job training plan) for housing and community development assistance for residents with the planning for housing and community development.</p>
